; spell/src/spell/define.bl — the ONE tool that grows the machine.
;
; The model proposes a whole `defcontract` or `defview`; this walks a validation
; ladder and either commits it or refuses with the reason. Rungs 1–2 are pure
; and live here:
;
;   1. schema   — is the proposal well-formed?
;   2. machine  — does it AGREE with everything registered so far?
;   3. compile  — verse's own diagnostics          (spell.define.verse, W4)
;   4. ghosts   — styled selectors nothing renders (spell.define.verse, W4)
;
; ── why one tool rather than define_contract + define_view ──────────────────
;
; The machine's invariant is that the two halves agree. A single entry point
; makes "you added a view whose binding no contract declares" the natural
; response, rather than an error raised by whichever tool happened to run
; second.
;
; ── why the proposal is JSON-shaped data, not source ────────────────────────
;
; The model sends a tool call, and its arguments are JSON. Accepting beam-lisp
; SOURCE instead would mean evaluating text a model wrote — the eval-in-image
; problem, which belongs behind a fence and a scratch node (recorded in
; PLAN-025 as later work), not in the path that renders a page. A proposal is
; therefore inert data, and this file's job is to turn it into the same terms a
; human author would have written by hand.
;
; ── the asymmetry, restated ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Orphan binding and unhandled fire are ERRORS: the page reads or fires a name
; nobody publishes or handles, so it is broken NOW. Unrendered assign and dead
; template are WARNINGS: a machine grown one definition at a time is supposed
; to pass through incomplete states, and a tool that refuses them cannot be
; used for the thing it exists to do.

(ns spell.define
  (:require [spell.machine :refer [empty-machine register-contract register-view
                                   views contracts report errors warnings
                                   view-bindings view-fires all-assigns]]
            [spell.contract :refer [parse parse-view]]
            [rewrite :refer [postwalk]]))

;; ── rung 0: the vocabulary ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

(def proposal-keys
  "Every key this namespace READS out of a proposal.

   The list is here, beside the `(get p :…)` calls it describes, because it is
   the same fact as those calls: a key this file never reads is a key a
   proposal has no business carrying, and a key it reads that is missing here
   is a field silently dropped at the boundary.

   ── what it is FOR ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

   A proposal arrives as JSON, so its keys are STRINGS written by a model, and
   `spell.define` reads them as KEYWORDS. Something has to convert, and the
   conversion is the security boundary of the whole design:

     * interning every incoming key would make the atom table — which is never
       collected and whose exhaustion aborts the VM uncatchably — writable by
       whoever is talking to the model;
     * printing the proposal as source and evaluating it was the OLD boundary,
       and it was open: a crafted KEY closed the call and opened a new form, so
       `(def pwned 99)` ran before any rung did (reproduced; commit e6a4828).

   So the converter admits a key only if it appears in this list, and every
   name here is a compile-time constant of this file — the keyword already
   exists before any model speaks. Nothing a model writes can add to the table.

   Unlisted keys are DROPPED, not refused: a model that includes a stray field
   has still described a definition, and refusing the whole proposal over a
   spare key trades a working definition for a pedantic one. What must never
   happen is the key being INTERNED or EVALUATED, and dropping achieves both.

   ── what is NOT here, deliberately ──────────────────────────────────────────

   The keys of a free-form map — CSS declarations under `:rules`, push field
   names under `:fields`. Those are open sets by definition (`font-size`,
   `--brand`, anything verse accepts), and they are read POSITIONALLY: the
   emitters walk `(keys m)` and print whatever is there, so a string key
   survives untouched. They stay strings, and therefore never reach the atom
   table at all.
   ── why KEYWORDS and not strings ─────────────────────────────────────────

   Spelling them as keywords is what MAKES the guarantee above true rather than
   merely intended. Loading this namespace interns every one of them, so by the
   time any proposal arrives the atom already exists and the converter's
   `String.to_existing_atom/1` can only ever find it — never create it.

   As strings, most of these atoms happened to exist anyway (`:kind` and
   `:name` are literals in the readers below) but `:rationale` did not: it is
   only ever built by `(keyword f)` at runtime inside `missing-fields`. The
   converter therefore could not intern it, silently left the key as a string,
   and rung 1 reported a proposal that HAD a rationale as missing one — with a
   correct-looking message naming the field the model had supplied. Found by a
   test that expected a rung-4 refusal and got a rung-1 one."
  [:kind :name :rationale :opts
   ;; contract half
   :assigns :events :pushes :type :params :replies :fields
   ;; view half
   :templates :html :style :selector :rules :binds
   ;; the three bind shapes
   :each :binding :as :template
   :on :event :fire :arg :value :key
   :view :arms])

;; ── rung 1: schema ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(def kinds ["contract" "view"])

(defn- missing-fields
  "The required fields absent from `p`, as strings."
  [p]
  (filterv (fn [f] (let [v (get p (keyword f))]
                     (or (nil? v) (= "" v))))
           ["kind" "name" "rationale"]))

(defn- schema-problem
  "The first schema violation in `p`, or nil.

   Returns ONE problem rather than all of them: the model retries with a whole
   new proposal, so a list of every fault in a proposal it is about to discard
   is noise. The one that stopped it is the one worth reading."
  [p]
  (let [missing (missing-fields p)]
    (cond
      (not (empty? missing))
      {:missing missing
       :message (str "a proposal needs " (join ", " missing)
                     " — rationale is what renders in the chat, so a change nobody "
                     "can read is a change nobody can review")}

      (not (some (fn [k] (= k (get p :kind))) kinds))
      {:unknown-kind (get p :kind)
       :message (str "kind must be one of " (join ", " kinds))}

      ;; A template with no html renders an empty element: it passes every
      ;; downstream check and shows the user a blank box.
      (some (fn [t] (or (nil? (get t :html)) (= "" (get t :html))))
            (get p :templates []))
      {:message "every template needs html — an empty one renders a blank element and passes every later check"}

      ;; A style rule with no selector cannot be joined against anything.
      (some (fn [s] (or (nil? (get s :selector)) (= "" (get s :selector))))
            (get p :style []))
      {:message "every style rule needs a selector"}

      (some (fn [b] (or (nil? (get b :selector)) (= "" (get b :selector))))
            (get p :binds []))
      {:message "every bind needs a selector"}

      :else nil)))

;; ── proposal → term ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;
;; These build the SAME shapes `(parse …)` and `(parse-view …)` produce from
;; hand-written source, so everything downstream — the machine joins, the EDN
;; emitters, verse — cannot tell a model's definition from an author's. That is
;; the point: one representation, one set of checks.

(defn- sym
  "A symbol from a string name."
  [s]
  (symbol (str s)))

(defn- deref-form
  "`@name` as the reader produces it: (deref name)."
  [nm]
  (list (quote deref) (sym nm)))

(defn- assign-clause [a]
  (list (quote assign)
        (deref-form (get a :name))
        (keyword (get a :type "any"))))

(defn- event-clause
  "An `(on :ev [params…] (ok \"…\"))` clause.

   The body is a bare `(ok …)` because a proposal declares an event's SHAPE,
   not its behaviour: what the handler does on the BEAM is server code, which a
   page cannot see and a model must not be able to write into the running image
   through a rendering tool. `reply-tags` still enumerates the arms, so the
   seam check keeps working."
  [e]
  (let [params (mapv sym (get e :params []))
        replies (get e :replies ["ok"])]
    (list (quote on)
          (keyword (get e :name))
          params
          (if (some (fn [r] (= r "err")) replies)
            (list (quote if) (quote true)
                  (list (quote ok) "accepted")
                  (list (quote err) "refused"))
            (list (quote ok) "accepted")))))

(defn- push-clause [p]
  (list (quote push)
        (deref-form (get p :name))
        (get p :fields {})))

(defn proposal->contract
  "A contract proposal as a contract term \u2014 identical in shape to `(parse …)`."
  [p]
  (parse (keyword (get p :name))
         (get p :opts {})
         (concat (mapv assign-clause (get p :assigns []))
                 (mapv push-clause (get p :pushes []))
                 (mapv event-clause (get p :events [])))))

(defn- template-form
  "One `(template &name [$params] \"<html>\")` clause.

   Parameters are taken from the proposal when it lists them, and DERIVED from
   the html when it does not.

   ── why derive ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

   A model proposed `{:name \"clockface\" :html \"<div>{@m.text}</div>\"}` with no
   `params`, and it was RIGHT to: the loop variable is named by the bind
   (`:as \"m\"`), so listing it again is redundant and a model reading the schema
   has no reason to. The emitter took that literally, emitted
   `@template &clockface() { … $m.text … }`, and verse compiled it happily
   because an unbound reference in a template body is not an error — it is a
   name that resolves to nothing.

   The result passed all four rungs and rendered `<div class='clock'></div>`
   twice: the right number of elements, all of them empty. Observed in a
   browser after the model's own definition was accepted. Every check agreed
   the page was correct; the page said nothing.

   So a template's parameters are derived from the names its body actually
   interpolates, which is the only source that cannot disagree with the body.
   An explicit `:params` still wins, for the case where a template takes an
   argument it does not interpolate directly."
  [t]
  (let [declared (get t :params [])
        params (if (empty? declared) (html-params (get t :html "")) declared)]
    (list (quote template)
          (sym (str "&" (get t :name)))
          (mapv (fn [prm] (sym (str "$" prm))) params)
          (get t :html))))

(defn- html-params
  "The distinct `@name` roots a template body interpolates, in first-seen order.

   `<div>{@m.text} {@m.role}</div>` → [\"m\"].

   Order is first-seen rather than sorted because a template's parameter list is
   POSITIONAL: `&t($m, $n)` binds by position, so a sort would silently swap two
   arguments the day a template takes two."
  [html]
  (let [hits (Regex/scan (Regex/compile! "\\{@([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*)") html)]
    (vec (distinct (mapv (fn [hit] (nth hit 1)) hits)))))

(defn- style-rule [s]
  [(get s :selector) (get s :rules {})])

(defn- bind-form
  "One bind, as the `binds` plane spells it.

   Three shapes, matching the three affordances a page actually uses:
     :each  \u2192 (st/each @xs :as @x :template &t)   render a collection
     :on    \u2192 (st/on :ev (st/value @x))           write a page-local
            \u2192 (st/on :ev (fire :name @arg))       cross the seam
     :view  \u2192 (st/view @x [\"arm\" &t])            reactive dispatch"
  [b]
  (let [sel (get b :selector)
        each (get b :each)
        on (get b :on)
        vw (get b :view)]
    (cond
      (not (nil? each))
      [sel (list (quote st/each)
                 (deref-form (get each :binding))
                 :as (deref-form (get each :as "item"))
                 :template (sym (str "&" (get each :template))))]

      (not (nil? on))
      ;; `:key` becomes a DRIVER PARAM, appended after the action.
      ;;
      ;; Without it a model cannot express "send on Enter" at all: it could
      ;; only propose a bare `:keydown`, which fires on EVERY keystroke and
      ;; sends one message per character. That is the exact defect the seed
      ;; view carries a comment about (verse BUG-375) — fixed there by hand and
      ;; unreachable here, so every model-proposed keyboard binding would have
      ;; re-created it.
      ;;
      ;; One param, not an open map: `:key` is the filter verse's grammar
      ;; actually has a slot for. A second one joins when there is a second
      ;; slot, and an unknown name would be silently discarded by verse — which
      ;; is the failure mode this whole file exists to refuse.
      (let [action (cond
                     (not (nil? (get on :fire)))
                     (let [arg (get on :arg)]
                       (if (nil? arg)
                         (list (quote fire) (keyword (get on :fire)))
                         (list (quote fire) (keyword (get on :fire)) (deref-form arg))))

                     (not (nil? (get on :value)))
                     (list (quote st/value) (deref-form (get on :value)))

                     :else
                     (throw (ex-info "define: an `on` bind must either fire or write a value"
                                     {:bind b})))
            head (list (quote st/on) (keyword (get on :event "click")) action)
            k (get on :key)]
        [sel (if (nil? k) head (concat head (list :key k)))])

      (not (nil? vw))
      ;; Arms are FLAT, not a list of pairs: `(st/view @x ["a" &t "b" &u])`.
      ;;
      ;; Emitting `[["a" &t]]` instead produced a page verse refused with
      ;; `E0946: Expected capture $arms:body but no tokens remaining` — found by
      ;; running the loop against a real model, whose proposal was CORRECT and
      ;; whose definition was rejected twice by my own emitter. The seed's
      ;; hand-written bind shows the shape: `(st/view @status ["thinking"
      ;; &thinking])`.
      [sel (list (quote st/view)
                 (deref-form (get vw :binding))
                 (vec (mapcat (fn [arm] [(get arm 0) (sym (str "&" (get arm 1)))])
                              (get vw :arms []))))]

      :else
      (throw (ex-info "define: a bind must carry :each, :on or :view" {:bind b})))))

(defn proposal->view
  "A view proposal as a view term \u2014 identical in shape to `(parse-view …)`."
  [p]
  (parse-view (keyword (get p :name))
              (list (cons (quote markup) (mapv template-form (get p :templates [])))
                    (cons (quote style) (mapv style-rule (get p :style [])))
                    (cons (quote binds) (mapv bind-form (get p :binds []))))))

;; ── page-locals: what keeps rung 2 honest ───────────────────────────────────

(defn view-signals
  "Page-locals the browser WRITES: the `@x` in `(st/on :ev (st/value @x))`.

   These are real signals living as long as the page, so a page must DECLARE
   them (`@data inline $x : \"\";`) or it references an undeclared name."
  [v]
  (let [acc (atom [])]
    (postwalk
      (fn [node]
        (when (and (seq? node) (= (first node) (quote st/value)) (= 2 (count node)))
          (let [b (second node)]
            (when (and (seq? b) (= (first b) (quote deref)))
              (swap! acc conj (name (second b))))))
        node)
      (get v :binds))
    (vec (sort (distinct @acc)))))

(defn view-scoped
  "Names bound per-iteration: the `@row` in `(st/each @xs :as @row …)`.

   Kept distinct from `view-signals` because the page must NOT declare these. A
   loop variable exists only inside the `@each` that binds it; declaring it as a
   page signal would create a second, permanently empty name that a template's
   `{@row.x}` could resolve to instead — a page that compiles and renders blank
   rows, which is exactly the class of failure this ladder exists to catch."
  [v]
  (let [acc (atom [])]
    (postwalk
      (fn [node]
        (when (and (seq? node) (= (first node) (quote st/each)))
          (loop [items (vec node) i 0]
            (when (< i (count items))
              (when (= (get items i) :as)
                (let [b (get items (+ i 1))]
                  (when (and (seq? b) (= (first b) (quote deref)))
                    (swap! acc conj (name (second b))))))
              (recur items (+ i 1)))))
        node)
      (get v :binds))
    (vec (sort (distinct @acc)))))

(defn view-locals
  "Every name this view introduces ITSELF — signals it writes plus loop variables
   it binds — and therefore every name no contract should declare.

   Both kinds are exempt from the orphan-binding check, which is why one
   function answers it. They are NOT interchangeable downstream: only
   `view-signals` may be emitted as page declarations.

   Derivation is the whole point. If locals arrived in the proposal, a model
   could silence any orphan-binding finding by listing the binding as local —
   the check would be fed its own answer, and `validate` would accept a page
   reading names nobody publishes. Measured on the real seed view, the naive
   binding scan returns [draft m messages status]: two of those four are local,
   so without this the seed's own view reports two orphans."
  [v]
  (vec (sort (distinct (concat (view-signals v) (view-scoped v))))))

;; ── rung 2: the machine joins ───────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn machine-findings
  "Errors and warnings for `m`, with this view's page-locals exempted.

   Locals are collected across EVERY registered view, not just the incoming
   one: a machine holding two views must not report the first view's local as
   an orphan while checking the second."
  [m]
  (let [locals (vec (distinct (mapcat view-locals (views m))))]
    {:errors (errors m locals)
     :warnings (warnings m)}))

;; ── the ladder ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- rejected [rung reason m]
  {:status :rejected :rung rung :reason reason :machine m :report (report m [])})

(defn- tentative
  "`m` with the proposal registered — the state to CHECK, not to keep.

   Checking a proposal in isolation would miss the questions that only exist
   once it joins the others, which is the entire reason the machine
   accumulates. So it is registered first and rolled back on refusal; because
   the registry is keyed by name, rollback is simply not keeping the result."
  [m p]
  (if (= "contract" (get p :kind))
    (register-contract m (proposal->contract p))
    (register-view m (proposal->view p))))

(defn validate
  "Walk the pure rungs. Returns {:status :ok|:rejected :rung … :reason …
   :warnings … :machine …} where `:machine` is the state that WOULD be
   committed (rung 1–2 only; the verse rungs live in spell.define.verse)."
  [m p]
  (let [problem (schema-problem p)]
    (if (not (nil? problem))
      (rejected :schema problem m)
      ;; A malformed bind throws while being built — a proposal naming neither
      ;; :each, :on nor :view, say. That is a schema fault discovered late, so
      ;; it is reported as one rather than crashing the loop.
      (let [built (try {:ok (tentative m p)}
                       (catch e {:err (str (ex-message e))}))]
        (if (not (nil? (get built :err)))
          (rejected :schema {:message (get built :err)} m)
          (let [m2 (get built :ok)
                found (machine-findings m2)]
            (if (not (empty? (get found :errors)))
              (rejected :machine (get found :errors) m)
              {:status :ok
               :machine m2
               :warnings (get found :warnings)
               :report (report m2 [])})))))))

(defn define
  "Add a contract or a view to the machine.

   Returns the same map `validate` does. On refusal `:machine` is the ORIGINAL,
   unchanged — a machine that half-accepts a broken definition is worse than
   one that refuses it, because every later proposal is then checked against a
   state no author ever approved."
  [m p]
  (validate m p))
